id #uuid "9075355b-7512-5362-8a9e-57357160d013" :question/math-academy-id "q-131014" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{6}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{6}={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $4x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})={x}^{2}+{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-131014/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131014"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65077"] :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-79770/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e26a4325-a8bf-5731-9904-388c229204ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79770/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "ed46b30c-1f0c-5457-9c44-574a8cbdbbf9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79770/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "9f47b88e-2ff4-568f-983e-97c6643af732" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79770/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "8488c4f3-09e9-5aa4-acff-956eda98c33e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79770/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "8afa32dd-1288-5351-9ae9-2498943892b9" :answer/type :answer.ty