bb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79775" :question/id #uuid "76c00a91-3d84-5b9d-a58e-5823a1a84973" :question/math-academy-id "q-79775" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-9y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=2x{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)=6{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)=-5{e}^{3x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=2x{e}^{3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=6{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=6.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=-5{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79775/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79775"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79777/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "dc2bfa3f-3d48-57cd-9c33-2fb81fe223fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79777/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "908715c5-59eb-5841-b320-ccc51945869f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/val